1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,825
Imagine singing for four strangers... who are not even looking at you.

2
00:00:09,225 --> 00:00:14,645
They sit with their backs turned. Your knees are shaking. Your only weapon is your voice.

3
00:00:15,045 --> 00:00:18,823
Then, BOOM. A big red button. A chair spins around.

4
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:28,888
That simple, slightly terrifying idea is called The Voice. And it was born in a little country famous for bicycles, tulips, and very tall people.

5
00:00:30,188 --> 00:00:34,776
Our story starts in the Netherlands, home of the TV maker John de Mol.

6
00:00:35,176 --> 00:00:43,327
A Dutch TV boss, Erland Galjaard, gave him a challenge: make a singing show that goes one step further than The X Factor.

7
00:00:43,727 --> 00:00:48,827
Most talent shows judged the whole package. The hair, the clothes, the dance moves.

8
00:00:49,227 --> 00:00:55,969
De Mol's idea was beautifully simple: what if the judges could not see the singer at all? Only hear them.

9
00:00:56,369 --> 00:00:58,483
The blind audition was born.

10
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:05,224
But blind how? A curtain? A screen? A paper bag over every judge's head?

11
00:01:05,624 --> 00:01:10,937
The answer came from singer and producer Roel van Velzen: chairs that turn around.

12
00:01:11,337 --> 00:01:17,077
Turn your back to the singer, listen, and if you love the voice, hit the button and spin to face them.

13
00:01:17,477 --> 00:01:24,220
Suddenly the judges were not judges anymore. They were coaches, and the coaches had to fight for the singers.

14
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:31,409
The Voice of Holland first aired on RTL 4 on September seventeenth, twenty ten.

15
00:01:31,809 --> 00:01:39,960
The first coaches were singers Jeroen van der Boom, Angela Groothuizen, Roel van Velzen, and the duo Nick and Simon.

16
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:48,767
After the blind auditions came the battles: two singers, one song, one winner. Then the live shows, where viewers at home voted.

17
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:54,843
In January twenty eleven, a young singer named Ben Saunders won the very first season.

18
00:01:55,243 --> 00:01:58,465
And Pip? Pip auditioned too. No chair turned.

19
00:01:59,765 --> 00:02:03,863
Here's the thing. TV companies all over the world were watching.

20
00:02:04,263 --> 00:02:08,317
Soon the show was sold abroad, and the red chairs packed their bags.

21
00:02:08,717 --> 00:02:13,839
Over the years, the format was licensed to dozens and dozens of countries.

22
00:02:15,139 --> 00:02:21,647
In April twenty eleven, The Voice arrived in America on NBC, hosted by Carson Daly.

23
00:02:22,047 --> 00:02:28,512
The coaches were superstars: Christina Aguilera, Adam Levine, Blake Shelton, and CeeLo Green.

24
00:02:28,912 --> 00:02:33,841
The very first American winner was Javier Colón, from Adam Levine's team.

25
00:02:34,241 --> 00:02:38,787
Pip tried again, in English this time. Nothing. Not even a wobble.

26
00:02:40,087 --> 00:02:43,672
In twenty thirteen, the chairs landed in the Philippines.

27
00:02:44,072 --> 00:02:54,292
The Voice of the Philippines on ABS-CBN had coaches Lea Salonga, Sarah Geronimo, Bamboo, and apl.de.ap, with Toni Gonzaga as host.

28
00:02:54,692 --> 00:03:01,669
The first winner was Mitoy Yonting, from Lea Salonga's team, a band singer who finally got his big break.

29
00:03:02,969 --> 00:03:11,099
In twenty fifteen, the British broadcaster ITV bought Talpa, for about three hundred and fifty-five million pounds up front.

30
00:03:12,399 --> 00:03:18,309
And everywhere the show went, people played it at home. Backs turned. Big imaginary buttons.

31
00:03:18,709 --> 00:03:21,868
Even for Pip. Finally, every chair turned.

32
00:03:22,268 --> 00:03:28,264
Because the idea was never about chairs. It was about one question: what if we just listened?
